de but we cannot spend  all our time  doing exploratory analysis at some point  we have to move and start doing other  things  the important idea is that all these  expectations that we have about the data  we should make them explicit  and when i say explicit i mean writing  things writing a piece of code that  checks those expectations  for example if we are assuming that we  should always have a user id  because that that's implicit in our code  well let's make that expectation uh  explicit before we run that piece of  code  so we write this test that checks that  we always have a user id  if our code is assuming that we only  have rock pop and jazz  let's make that uh that explicit and  test that  what goes into that piece of code only  has those values that it  doesn't in our case that it doesn't have  the unknown value  and this this same idea applies if you  are using sql  even if you your project is written as  written in python you may be interacting  with a database  and your data exists in a database it's  going to be much faster just to run  these things directly in sql  so you can also apply these ideas in  different languages  now i i spoke about testing our  expectations about the data  but we are also writing code and we  should make sure that our code  works as expected so this is a different  type of testing which is called unit  testing  and this scenario happens in our  pipeline assuming we have our day  expectations as  tests this happens when data is expected  but the output from our code  is incorrect the idea here is that if we  have a let's say we have a function that  cleans uh  some some data we should break down this  big function that performs several  operations  into smaller functions that have really  concrete behaviors so that we can come  up with test cases easily  uh and the idea is that  we go from something that is kind of  abstract like and like  has a lot of things going on to  something that is very specific  and we isolate a lot of things and this  makes  testing easier and it's also important  to think in terms of what makes a good  unit test  you are going to be testing cases that  are representative of what you  are going to see when you analyze the  data and it's really easy to fall into  this trap  where you call unit cases that don't  make much sense so i'm gonna give an  example of  how you can think about unit testing  let's imagine we have a function  inside our clean songs  function we have another function that  does a  count operation so it's going to count  the number of songs for every genre for  each user  and this function is called count song  genres  and the piece of code that you see on  the right is  written using the pi test library which  is a library that i highly recommend for  writing unit tests so we have two tests  here  as we can see in the code snippet we  have the complete case this is a user  with id number one  and this person has listened to a rock  song a pop song and a jazz  song so the output is gonna be uh three  columns  rock pop and jazz and the values are  going to be one one one  now this is uh a good scenario from  someone who's been listening to these  three genres  now it may happen that someone doesn't  listen to the three genres  at all but we should still be counting  correctly  so let's imagine we have another user id  user ideas number two but this person  only likes rock and pop  well we expect to see  the just column as output so that's  something that we should be testing  and as you can see in the body of the  function what we are doing is we are  calling the function with those two  input cases  and we are making sure that we have the  right  set of columns now this is a very simple  test and you could test  other things such as the actual values  in the column but this is the important  idea that you should be testing for  concrete things  and also think in terms of uh scenarios  where  that you should be covering  so now let's speak about a recipe for  writing data transformations and  this is a recipe that if you follow step  by step is going to help you to write  more robust data transformations  let's imagine we have we are working on  the counseling genres function  the first thing even if before we called  anything we just write the name of the  function  we write the docs string and a docstring  is just a string that  goes right below the name of the  function  and that explains our purpose for for  this piece of code it's  basically a justification for why do we  have this function  and it's this is very important not only  for other people but also for you  because uh like one or two weeks from  now we are going to forget why you have  that piece of code there  so that's step number one step number  two is to think  what am i expecting about the data what  am i expecting in terms of the input for  this function and we should be writing  the data tests the expectation tests  the third step is to think what  scenarios  should my code cover which are the cases  where the  the data is expected and i should be  operating and applying some  transformations  those are uh that's kind of the  reasoning that you should follow for  thinking  for good unit test cases and finally  you code your function you run your  tests  and keep going until all your tests pass  and that's how you you make sure that  your implementation is correct  at least uh by taking into account how  many scenarios you tested with your  integration test and your unit tests  let's speak about the second challenge  data pipeline  structure our data analysis  goes through several stations that have  a predefined order  and this has a really important  implication when we are testing  code because it means that any error  that happens in  let's see this pipeline we have two  branches for analyzing the user's data  and the generals  let's imagine we are working with the  user's data if we get the data  and something bad happens there all  those errors are going to propagate  to all the downstream tasks in this case  all these errors are going to propagate  to clean users  and potentially even to the code that  trains the model  so when we are doing testing it's very  important that we account for this  fact so now let's revisit  our idea of data expectations and data  testing and turn this into  another idea a very similar idea which  is integration testing  it's the same thing we are going to make  explicit our expectations about the data  but now we are saying how and where  we are going to make these tests and the  idea is very simple  you have your pipeline and then right  after you run  your task for example get users you test  you run your data expectations so for  every task in your pipeline  you are going to have one function the  function that runs the actual code and  then another function the function that  tests  the output and so so that you can  explicitly test  your expectations about data  now if you are analyzing a big data set  or any of these tasks  takes a lot of time to run running your  integration testing  is going to take a lot of time because  that implies running your pipeline from  start to finish  and as i mentioned before speed is very  important so how do we  go about that how do we go about speed  that is actually very simple  since your pipeline operates in many  batches of data  and applies the same transformations too  many rows at once  we can simply pass a sample and if we if  we pass a smaller amount of data  the pipeline is going to process it in a  much faster so this is a really simple  idea  but you it implies some work from you  because it means that you have to code  your pipeline as a structural chain of  uh  chain of transformations and you have to  make sure that your data expectation  tests  run right after every task and there are  a few  uh a few tools that you can use  they are called workflow management  tools that you can use to turn your code  into a pipeline  my recommendation is to use plumber  which is a library that i've developed  and it it applies this idea and it  allows you to write pipelines very  quickly so that's what you see  on the left side this is one example of  how you can develop a pipeline as you  can see it's just a simple  uh jaml file where you say  uh the source is gonna be this script  for get users and when this task is done  i wanna run  this function which is defined in  another file and i'm going to pass one  parameter to this function which is  going to be sample and this is going to  allow me to run the entire thing  with the sample and we just repeat the  same thing for all the tasks that we  have we say  where's the source code what function we  want to execute when the task finishes  and in this way we can quickly switch  sampling on and off  so that we when we want to run our  integration testing quickly we just say  run the pipeline with sampling on and  when we want to see  the actual output with the full data set  we just turn the sampling off  there are a few other options and things  that you can do to speed up your  integration testing even more one  great idea is to do incremental builds  let's say for example that you  in this pipeline you modify clean users  if you modify clean users  you don't have to run get users again  you also don't have to run get generous  or clean generous  you only have to run clean users and  train models so running your integration  testing is much faster because you are  running  less fewer tasks  the second idea is to run tasks in  parallel uh  we can see in this pipeline we have two  branches that are independent and they  meet until you train a model so you can  run these tasks at the same time and you  can  save a lot of time by doing this and all  and these ideas are already implemented  in bloomberg so  it's very easy for you to get  integration testing  to speed up really fast  now let's speak about the last challenge  which is data changes  even if you are doing all these testing  and all these things and you are taking  proper care  of all these ideas if  the data that you are consuming changes  your pipeline is probably going to break  so  we have to think about how do we  approach this problem well the first  practical thing to notice is that  covering  all possible input cases is unrealistic  we are not going to cover all possible  uh  data input input cases because we simply  don't have enough time  we cannot just spend all our time  testing and i'm not doing the actual  work which is the analysis  so that's a very important thing  my recommendation is to focus on writing  data tests  because those are really fast uh  to write you just have you simply have  some ideas about the data  and you said that you write that in code  and it's  one or two lines it's very easy to write  and if the  the data tests don't pass well uh you  just have to  go and revisit your expectations but  this gives you  you are kind of reducing the scope of  your code and saying i only want my code  to work under these conditions  so in this way you have to you are  coding  simpler functions and you are only going  to increase the complexity  if you see something in the data that  requires you to do so  this testing trade-off is very important  to keep in mind  might in one end you want to be  writing a lot of tests so that you uh  can make sure that your data pipeline is  robust  on the other hand you cannot simply keep  testing over and over again  and this also has a important  implications in terms of the nature of  data science  because the the most important thing  when you are working a data science  project is to try a lot of things  ahead of time you don't know if some  pre-processing method is going to work  or not  so a lot of the code that you write is  not going to end up in production and  you are just going to throw it away so  it's not worth your time to test and  test and test things that you are not  going to use but there's a trade-off  right because what happens if you  implement some piece of code that you  that doesn't uh work but it doesn't work  not because of the  actual process but because you don't  implement it correctly  so as you get more experience you are  going to  have more confidence in balance work to  balance this threshold and  it's very important to keep this this  idea in mind  and the final um the final idea is  called reusability  if you have less code there are fewer  things that you have to test  so this is very important and this has  uh  two two different implications the first  one is that you should be using  other packages for example if you are  doing machine learning  don't implement your own model just use  another library like scikit-learn  or keras and also keep in mind that you  should be using  libraries that are maintained because  that's if you are using  libraries that are no longer maintained  that's going to have a whole lot set of  different issues  but the second thing also implies that  you should  reuse not only the code from other  packages but your own code  if you find yourself that you are  writing uh several functions that  perform similar things  it's a good idea to write a single  function that maybe has some parameters  to tweak  how it behaves and in this way you only  have to test a single function  as opposed to testing five functions  that do pretty much the same thing  now um this these are good ideas for  development but when you are going to  take your project  to production uh you have a different  set of concerns that you should be aware  of  the first important thing is that you  should define an unambiguous input  schema definition  and this is really just a few data  expectations at the beginning of your  pipeline because  you are saying if my pipeline is going  to production i'm going to be making new  predictions i should define what my  expectations about the input are  there's no point in running your  pipeline if the input doesn't uh  has it it doesn't have this specific  format when you are going to deploy  it's important that you heavily invest  in unit testing  because you have to cover more use cases  when you are developing you can  spend more time trying out new  experiments and writing data tests  but when you are going to deploy to  production you have to take care that  you cover more cases that  shouldn't raise errors for example and  speaking about errors is very important  that you have good error messages so  that you can  debug things and if something goes wrong  you are quickly able to understand  what's going on  and related to having good error  messages is the idea of vlogging  if you have good logging it's going to  be easier for you to understand what  happened  if something breaks and there's also um  this idea data science products when  they go to production they usually  are deployed either in batch batch  predictions for example you make  predictions for all your users every  month  or as live api api where you would have  a service  uh that can make predictions on demand  for everyone who  wants to get a prediction and for the  first case for the batch case  you are kind of in development mode  right because you don't have that  pressure if  uh you make predictions monthly and  something goes wrong  well you can just delay the predictions  for one or two days and fix things  for a live api the requirements are much  um strict because you have to make sure  that you get a proper response to  whoever is going to consume  the service that you are working on  now let's uh go to debugging strategy  this is very important because  when you find errors you have to follow  a principal the strategy so that you  fix the errors and they don't come up  again  let's speak about fixing crashes what  happens when you run your pipeline and  it crashes  there are three scenarios and  understanding which scenario  uh the case that you are dealing with  falls on  you have to do some detective work the  first case is unexpected data  and this happens when you see that one  of your data tests  crashes and this is actually a good  thing because it means that you  cut something that shouldn't go through  your pipeline that  doesn't uh comply with your data  expectations  then if you find that you are in this  case you either have to relax  your data expectations maybe you have to  revisit the definition of your tests  or leave some data out maybe you keep  that's for you to decide maybe you  decide to keep your tests  as they are but in the previous task you  are going to remove  some data that shouldn't go through your  pipeline  and if you do this just make sure that  you have a comment to explain  why you are leaving some observations  out from processing  the second case is unexpected data  but when the task crashes not the data  expectation  the code in the task  this implies that you have a missing  data test  because the tasks shouldn't crash if  your data is expected  right so the the idea is that you should  be adding  a data test then run your pipeline  make sure that your um pipeline crashes  at that point and then you're back to  case one  uh the third case is incorrect code so  again your task crashes  but it's um it's not it is not computing  what you are expecting it  to do so this means that you have a  missing  unit test so you should add this unit  test  um see it fail see that the current  implementation does not work  then fix it run the test and see that  it passes one important idea that you  should keep in mind is that you should  be fixing  problems in the right place imagine that  you  find your training model procedure is  crashing  and maybe it's some of the  rows for the users that have some issue  which is  causing your training model to crash you  might be tempted to fix  apply this fix in your trained model  code  the first question that you should ask  yourself is who is resp  which task is responsible for this  output and maybe you realize that  this is really something that clean  users should take care of  right so you should shouldn't apply uh  the fixed  wherever you find it because you you  maybe have to fix it in a different  place where  it is more appropriate to apply the  change and this is important for  tasks whose output is used by many other  tasks  if you apply the same the fix  in the root cause in the task that  should be responsible for that piece of  data you only have to apply that patch  that fix  once instead of in every task that uses  that output the other scenario which is  harder to fix  are silent bugs and this usually happens  for example when you train a model  and you realize that it doesn't have  doesn't have the performance that you  were expecting  or you compute some summary statistics  and the numbers don't make sense  so there's nothing crashes it's it's  just that  our results don't make sense so this  indicates a missing  unit or data test and again this is some  detective  work that you have to do to know if it's  one case or the other  and to fix this kind of issues you once  you  find something that doesn't make sense  you should work  backwards to find the same idea right so  who is responsible who should be  responsible for this piece of data  then you are the missing unit or data  test  depending on the case and then you apply  the same logic that we explained before  for fixing crashes so you basically  convert your  silent bug in an actual crash  and then you fix it  now i presented a lot of ideas and this  slide contains really  the most important messages from this  talk and that's what you  what i want you to take away from this  the first  important thing is that testing makes  reproducibility practical  unit tests check concrete expected  behavior in a  unit code in a piece of code  which is usually a single function  integration tests check input output  boundaries  so these are uh the tests that are going  to verify  that the output from some task is a is  an appropriate  input for the next task  you can speed up your integration  testing with sampling  you have to be prepared for data changes  you  should be writing your tests before you  actually write your code  and that you should be fixing box in the  right place  and you should be asking where it is the  best  place to fix this thing who which task  should be responsible  responsible for this this output tools  and resources  these are the tools that i recommend you  to use to start applying these  principles the first one is plumber  which is library that i  mentioned before and i'm developing it  allows you to write data pipelines  easily and it has support for  integration testing  you can also parameterize your pipeline  so so you switch the  sampling parameter on and off you can  you get incremental builds and parallel  execution so you can speed up your  integration testing even more it also  integrates with jupiter which means you  can develop your pipelines in an  interactive way  for your unit tests i recommend you to  use pi test it's a very powerful library  uh spend some time understanding how it  works and you'll be writing very concise  powerful unit tests  in no time finally a tool that i  recently discovered nox  this library allows you to recreate your  virtual environment before you run your  tests and this is uh this is great  making dependence installation part of  your testing procedure because sometimes  we forget to document dependencies we  just do  a pip install but we never uh keep a  record of these things so if you don't  document your dependencies  things may break so if you are  installing your dependencies every time  your test  you you'll know when these things  happened  and finally some resources if you want  to take a look at the slides you can get  them from  there any questions feedback about this  talk or plumber or anything  reach out via